# sent_id = beowulf.00033
# text = Wæs sēo hwīl micel; twelf wintra tīd torn geþolode wine Scyldinga, wēana gehwelcne, sīdra sorga; forðām [secgum] wearð, ylda bearnum undyrne cūð gyddum geōmore, þætte Grendel wan hwīle wið Hrōþgār, hetenīðas wæg, fyrene ond fǣhðe fela missēra, singāle sæce; sibbe ne wolde wið manna hwone mægenes Deniga, feorhbealo feorran, fēa þingian, nē þǣr nǣnig witena wēnan þorfte beorhtre bōte tō banan folmum; (ac se) ǣglǣca ēhtende wæs, deorc dēaþscua, duguþe ond geogoþe, seomade ond syrede; sinnihte hēold mistige mōras; men ne cunnon, hwyder helrūnan hwyrftum scrīþað.
# ref = 146-163
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = The time was long; for twelve winters' time the friend of the Scyldings endured affliction, every woe, deep sorrows; therefore it became openly known to men, to the children of men, sadly in songs, that Grendel strove for a long time against Hrothgar, carried on hostile attacks, crimes and feud for many half-years, continual conflict; he would not make peace with any man of the Danish host, would not remove deadly evil for a payment, nor did any of the counsellors there need to expect splendid compensation from the slayer's hands; but the adversary, the dark death-shadow, was persecuting old retainers and young warriors, lurked and lay in ambush; in perpetual night he held the misty moors; men do not know whither hellish adepts wander in their roamings.
# literal_translation = Was the time great; twelve winters' time torment suffered the friend of the Scyldings, every woe, deep sorrows; therefore [to men] became, to the children of men manifest known in songs sadly, that Grendel strove for a time against Hrothgar, enmities carried on, crimes and feud for many half-years, continual conflict; peace not would with any man of the host of the Danes, deadly evil remove, for payment settle, nor there any of counsellors expect needed of brighter compensation from the slayer's hands; but the adversary persecuting was, dark death-shadow, old retainers and young warriors, lurked and ambushed; in perpetual night held misty moors; men not know, whither hell-runes in roamings wander.
