# sent_id = beowulf.00062
# text = Ic þæs Hrōðgār mæg þurh rūmne sefan rǣd gelǣran, hū hē frōd ond gōd fēond oferswȳðeþ -- gyf him edwenden ǣfre scolde bealuwa bisigu bōt eft cuman --, ond þā cearwylmas cōlran wurðaþ; oððe ā syþðan earfoðþrāge, þrēanȳd þolað, þenden þǣr wunað on hēahstede hūsa sēlest.'
# ref = 277-285
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = I, Hrothgar, can with a generous mind teach counsel, how he, wise and good, may overcome the enemy — if ever a reversal of evils, a remedy for affliction, were to come again to him —, and those seethings of sorrow become cooler; or forever afterwards he endures a time of anguish, distress, while he remains there in the high place, the best of houses.
# literal_translation = I, Hrothgar, can through a generous mind teach advice, how he, wise and good, overcomes the enemy — if to him a reversal ever should come afterwards as a remedy for the affliction of evils —, and those seethings of sorrow become cooler; or forever afterwards a time of anguish, distress, he endures, while there he remains in the high place, the best of houses.
