# sent_id = beowulf.00097
# text = þā mē þæt gelǣrdon lēode mīne, þā sēlestan, snotere ceorlas, þēoden Hrōðgār, þæt ic þē sōhte, forþan hīe mægenes cræft mīn[n]e cūþon; selfe ofersāwon, ðā ic of searwum cwōm, fāh from fēondum, þǣr ic fīfe geband, ȳðde eotena cyn, ond on ȳðum slōg niceras nihtes, nearoþearfe drēah, wræc Wedera nīð -- wēan āhsodon -- forgrand gramum; ond nū wið Grendel sceal, wið þām āglǣcan āna gehēgan ðing wið þyrse.
# ref = 415-426
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then my people, the best ones, wise men, lord Hrothgar, advised me that I should seek you, because they knew the strength of my might; they themselves looked on when I came from battle, blood-stained from enemies, where I bound five, destroyed the race of giants, and on the waves struck water-monsters by night, suffered severe distress, avenged the affliction of the Weders -- they asked for troubles -- I crushed the hostile ones; and now against Grendel, against that adversary, I alone must hold a meeting with the giant.
# literal_translation = Then me that advised men mine, the best, wise men, lord Hrothgar, that I you sought, because they of strength might mine knew; themselves looked on, when I from battle came, blood-stained from enemies, where I five bound, destroyed of giants race, and on waves struck water-monsters by night, severe distress suffered, avenged of Weders affliction -- troubles asked for -- crushed the hostile ones; and now with Grendel shall, with the adversary alone hold meeting with giant.
