# reading_translation = Unferth spoke, the son of Ecglaf, who sat at the feet of the lord of the Scyldings, he unbound a battle-rune — to him Beowulf's undertaking, the courageous seafarer's, was a great vexation, because he did not grant that any other man ever should obtain more glorious deeds in the world under the heavens than he himself —: 'Are you that Beowulf, who contended with Breca, competed in swimming on the wide sea, where you two for daring made trial of the waters and for foolish boast ventured your lives in the deep water?'
# literal_translation = Unferth spoke, Ecglaf's son, who at feet sat of lord of Scyldings, unbound battle-rune — was to him Beowulf's undertaking, of the courageous seafarer, great vexation, because he not granted, that any other man ever of glorious deeds the more of the world should obtain under heavens than he himself —: 'Are you that Beowulf, who with Breca strove, on wide sea about swimming competed, where you two for daring waters made trial of and for foolish boast in deep water lives ventured?'
