# sent_id = beowulf.00141
# text = Secge ic þē tō sōðe, sunu Ecglāfes, þæt nǣfre Gre[n]del swā fela gryra gefremede, atol ǣglǣca ealdre þīnum, hȳnðo on Heorote, gif þīn hige wǣre, sefa swā searogrim, swā þū self talast; ac hē hafað onfunden, þæt hē þā fǣhðe ne þearf, atole ecgþræce ēower lēode swīðe onsittan, Sige-Scyldinga; nymeð nȳdbāde, nǣnegum ārað lēode Deniga, ac hē lust wigeð, swefeð ond sendeþ, secce ne wēneþ tō Gār-Denum.
# ref = 590-601
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = I say to you in truth, son of Ecglaf, that Grendel would never have inflicted so many horrors, terrible adversary, upon your lord, humiliations in Heorot, if your heart, your spirit, were as fierce in battle as you yourself suppose; but he has found out that he need not greatly dread the feud, the terrible sword-storm, of your people, the Victory-Scyldings; he takes an enforced toll, spares no one of the Danish people, but he has his pleasure, kills and sends away, expects no fight from the Spear-Danes.
# literal_translation = Say I to you in truth, son of Ecglaf, that never Grendel so many horrors inflicted, terrible adversary, on lord your, humiliations in Heorot, if your heart were, spirit as battle-fierce, as you yourself suppose; but he has found out, that he the feud not need, terrible sword-storm your people greatly dread, Victory-Scyldings; takes enforced toll, no one spares of people of Danes, but he pleasure has, kills and sends, fight not expects from Spear-Danes.
