# sent_id = beowulf.00160
# text = Gespræc þā se gōda gylpworda sum, Bēowulf Gēata, ǣr hē on bed stige: 'Nō ic mē an herewæsmun hnāgran talige gūþgeweorca, þonne Grendel hine; forþan ic hine sweorde swebban nelle, aldre benēotan, þēah ic eal mæge; nāt hē þāra gōda, þæt hē mē ongēan slea, rand gehēawe, þēah ðe hē rōf sīe nīþgeweorca; ac wit on niht sculon secge ofersittan, gif hē gesēcean dear wīg ofer wǣpen, ond siþðan wītig God on swā hwæþere hond hālig Dryhten mǣrðo dēme, swā him gemet þince.'
# ref = 675-687
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then the good one, Beowulf of the Geats, spoke some boastful words before he climbed into bed: 'I do not consider myself at all poorer in martial strength or warlike deeds than Grendel himself; therefore I will not kill him with a sword, deprive him of life, though I fully can. He does not know the skills, so that he may strike back at me, hew my shield, though he may be strong in hostile deeds. But we two shall forgo the sword tonight, if he dares seek war without weapon, and then let wise God, the holy Lord, adjudge glory to whichever hand seems fitting to him.'
# literal_translation = Spoke then the good one of boasting-words some, Beowulf of Geats, before he on bed stepped: 'Not I me in martial-vigour poorer consider of war-deeds, than Grendel himself; therefore I him with sword will-not kill, of life deprive, though I all can; knows not he of-the skills, that he me against strike, shield hew, though he strong be of hostile-deeds; but we-two in night shall sword forgo, if he seek dares war without weapon, and afterwards wise God on so whichever hand holy Lord glory adjudge, as to-him fitting seems.'
