# sent_id = beowulf.00237
# text = Ðā wæs swīgra secg, sunu Ec[g]lāfes, on gylpsprǣce gūðgeweorca, siþðan æþelingas eorles cræfte ofer hēanne hrōf hand scēawedon, fēondes fingras; foran ǣghwylc wæs, stī[r]a nægla gehwylc stȳle gelīcost, hǣþenes handsporu hilderinces egl[u] unhēoru; ǣghwylc gecwæð, þæt him heardra nān hrīnan wolde īren ǣrgōd, þæt ðæs āhlǣcan blōdge beadufolme onberan wolde.
# ref = 980-990
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then the man, the son of Ecglaf, was more silent in boasting speech about warlike deeds, after the princes, by the warrior's strength, examined the hand up on the high roof, the enemy's fingers; each one in front was, each of the nails, most like steel, the heathen's claw, the warrior's hideous and monstrous hand; each one said that no hard, proven-good iron sword would touch him, in such a way that it would injure the adversary's bloody battle-hand.
# literal_translation = Then was more silent the man, son of Ecglaf, in boasting-speech of war-deeds, after princes by the warrior's strength over the high roof the hand examined, the enemy's fingers; in front each was, of nails each, steel most like, the heathen's claw of the warrior hideous, monstrous; each said that to him of hard ones none touch would iron proven-good, that of the adversary the bloody battle-hand injure would.
