# sent_id = beowulf.00369
# text = Gyrede hine Bēowulf eorlgewǣdum, nalles for ealdre mearn; scolde herebyrne hondum gebrōden, sīd ond searofāh sund cunnian, sō ðe bāncofan beorgan cūþe, þæt him hildegrāp hreþre ne mihte, eorres inwitfeng aldre gesceþðan; ac se hwīta helm hafelan werede, sē þe meregrundas mengan scolde, sēcan sundgebland since geweorðad, befongen frēawrāsnum, swā hine fyrndagum worhte wǣpna smið, wundrum tēode, besette swīnlīcum, þæt hine syðþan nō brond nē beadomēcas bītan ne meahton.
# ref = 1441-1454
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Beowulf armed himself in warrior's gear; he did not at all feel anxiety for his life. The war-mail woven by hand, broad and cunningly decorated, was to explore the water, so that it could protect his bone-chamber, so that the battle-grip could not injure his breast, the malicious grasp of the angry one could not harm his life. But the shining helmet protected his head, the one that was to stir up the pool's bottom, seek the surging water, honoured with treasure, encircled by splendid chains, as the smith of weapons had made it in days of yore, wondrously fashioned it, adorned it with boar-images, so that afterwards no sword nor battle-swords could bite it.
# literal_translation = Girded himself Beowulf with warrior's-clothing, not-at-all for life mourned; should war-mail with-hands woven, broad and cunningly-decorated water explore, so that bone-chamber protect could, that him battle-grip breast not might, angry malicious-grasp life injure; but the white helmet head protected, that which pool-bottoms stir should, seek surging-water with treasure honoured, encircled with splendid-chains, as it in days-of-yore made weapons' smith, wondrously fashioned, adorned with boar-images, that it afterwards never sword nor battle-swords bite not might.
