# sent_id = beowulf.00393
# text = Līxte se lēoma, lēoht inne stōd, efne swā of hefene hādre scīneð rodores candel Hē æfter recede wlāt; hwearf þā be wealle, wǣpen hafenade heard be hiltum Higelāces ðegn yrre ond anrǣd,-- næs sēo ecg fracod hildetince, ac hē hraþe wolde Grendle forgyldan gūðrǣsa fela ðāra þe hē geworhte tō West-Denum oftor micle ðonne on ǣnne sīð, þonne hē Hrōðgāres heorðgenēatas slōh on sweofote, slǣpende frǣt folces Denigea fȳftȳne men, ond ōðer swylc ūt offerede, lāðlicu lāc.
# ref = 1570-1584
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = The light shone, brightness stood within, just as the candle of the sky shines brightly from heaven. He looked along the hall; then he turned by the wall, the retainer of Hygelac raised the hard weapon by its hilt, angry and resolute — the blade was not useless to the warrior — but he quickly wished to repay Grendel for the many attacks that he had made on the West-Danes, far more often than on a single occasion, when he struck Hrothgar's hearth-companions in their sleep, devoured fifteen sleeping men of the Danish people, and carried off as many others, a terrible booty.
# literal_translation = Shone the light, brightness within stood, even as from heaven brightly shines the sky's candle. He along the hall looked; turned then by the wall, weapon raised hard by hilts Hygelac's thane, angry and resolute — was not the blade useless to the warrior — but he quickly would to Grendel repay of war-attacks many of those that he wrought to West-Danes oftener much than on one occasion, when he Hrothgar's hearth-companions struck in sleep, sleeping devoured of the people of Danes fifteen men, and other such out carried off, terrible booty.
