# sent_id = beowulf.00459
# text = Hafast þū gefēred, þæt þām folcum sceal, Gēata lēodum ond Gār-Denum sib gemǣne, ond sacu restan, inwitnīþas, þē hīe ǣr drugon, wesan, þenden ic wealde wīdan rīces, māþmas gemǣne, manig ōþerne gōdum gegrēttan ofer ganotes bæð sceal hringnaca ofer heafu bringan lāc ond luftācen.
# ref = 1855-1863
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = You have brought it about that peace shall be shared by those peoples, the Geatish people and the Spear-Danes, and that strife, hostile acts which they formerly practiced, shall rest, while I rule the wide kingdom, shared treasures, that many a one shall greet another with gifts; across the gannet's bath the ring-prowed ship shall bring gifts and love-tokens over the seas.
# literal_translation = You have brought about, that for those peoples, for the Geat people and for the Spear-Danes, peace shall be shared, and strife shall rest, hostile acts which they before practiced, while I rule of the wide kingdom, treasures shared, many another with gifts shall greet; across the gannet's bath shall the ring-prowed ship across the seas bring gifts and love-token.
