# text = Bīowulf maðelode, bearn Ecgðīoes: 'þæt is undyrne, dryhten Higelāc, (micel) gemēting, monegum fīra, hwylc (orleg)hwīl uncer Grendles wearð on ðām wange, þǣr hē worna fela Sige-Scyldingum sorge gefremede, yrmðe tō aldre; ic ðæt eall gewræc, swā begylpan [ne] þearf Grendeles māga (ǣnig) ofer eorðan ūhthlem þone, sē ðe lengest leofað lāðan cynnes, f(ācne) bifongen.
# ref = 1999-2009
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Beowulf, son of Ecgtheow, spoke: 'That is manifest, lord Hygelac, the great encounter, to many men, what a hostile time of ours and Grendel's came to be in that place, where he inflicted a great many sorrows and miseries for all time on the Victory-Scyldings; I avenged all that, so that no kinsman of Grendel on earth need boast of that night-uproar, any one of the hateful race who lives longest, enveloped in crime.'
# literal_translation = Beowulf spoke, son of Ecgtheow: 'That is manifest, lord Hygelac, (great) encounter, to many of men, what (hostile) time of us two and Grendel became in that place, where he a great many of sorrows to Victory-Scyldings inflicted, of miseries for life; I that all avenged, so that boast [not] need of Grendel kinsman (any) over earth of that night-uproar, he who longest lives of hateful race, (criminal) enveloped.'
