# sent_id = beowulf.00523
# text = Hēt ðā in beran eafor hēafodsegn, heaðostēapne helm, hāre byrnan, gūðsweord geatolīc, gyd æfter wræc: Mē ðis hildesceorp Hrōðgār sealde, snotra fengel; sume worde hēt, þæt ic his ǣrest ðē ēst gesægde; cwæð þæt hyt hæfde Hiorogār cyning, lēod Scyldunga lange hwīle; nō ðȳ ǣr suna sīnum syllan wolde, hwatum Heorowearde, ðēah hē him hold wǣre, brēostgewǣdu.
# ref = 2152-2162
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then he ordered the boar-head banner, the battle-towering helmet, the grey mail-coat, the splendid war-sword to be brought in; he uttered a speech thereupon: 'Hrothgar, the wise prince, gave me this war-gear; with a certain word he ordered that I should first tell you his good will; he said that Heorogar, king, man of the Scyldings, had it for a long time; yet he would not sooner give the breast-garments to his son, the bold Heoroweard, though he was loyal to him.'
# literal_translation = Ordered then in bear boar head-sign, battle-towering helmet, grey mail-coat, war-sword splendid, speech after uttered: Me this war-clothing Hrothgar gave, wise prince; some word ordered, that I his first you good will say; said that it had Heorogar king, man of Scyldings long time; not yet sooner son his give would, bold Heoroweard, though he him loyal were, breast-garments.
