# sent_id = beowulf.00558
# text = Hordweard sōhte georne æfter grunde, wolde guman findan þone þe him on sweofore sāre gerēode; hāt ond hrēohmōd hlǣw oft ymbehwearf ealne ūtanweard; nē ðǣr ǣnig mon on þǣre wēstenne,-- hwæðre wīges gefeh, bea(du)[we] weorces; hwīlum on beorh æthwearf, sincfæt sōhte; hē þæt sōna onfand, ðæt hæfde gumena sum goldes gefandod, hēahgestrēona.
# ref = 2293-2302
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = The guardian of the hoard searched eagerly along the ground, wishing to find the man who had sorely deprived him in the darkness; hot and angry in mind, he often circled the barrow all around the outside; nor was there any man in that wilderness — yet he rejoiced in battle, in the work of war; at times he turned back to the barrow, sought the precious cup; he immediately discovered that some one of men had tampered with the gold, the splendid treasures.
# literal_translation = Hoard-guardian sought eagerly after ground, wanted man to find that one who him in darkness sorely had deprived; hot and angry-minded barrow often circled all outward; nor there any man in that wilderness — however of war rejoiced, of battle-work; at times to barrow turned back, precious-cup sought; he that immediately discovered, that had of men some of gold tampered-with, of splendid-treasures.
