# sent_id = beowulf.00570
# text = Oferhogode ðā hringa fengel, þæt hē þone wīdflogan weorode gesōhte, sīdan herge; nō hē him þa sæcce ondrēd, nē him þæs wyrmes wīg for wiht dyde, eafoð ond ellen, forðon hē ǣr fela nearo nēðende nīða gedīgde, hildehlemma, syððan hē Hrōðgāres, sigorēadig secg, sele fǣlsode, ond æt gūðe forgrāp Grendeles mǣgum lāðan cynnes.
# ref = 2345-2354
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then the prince of rings disdained to seek out the wide-flyer with a troop, with a great army; he did not fear the fight for himself, nor did he regard the serpent's prowess as anything, its strength and courage, because he had before survived many assaults, brave in straits, clashes of battle, after he, the victory-blessed man, had cleansed Hrothgar's hall, and in battle had destroyed Grendel's hateful race of kinsmen.
# literal_translation = Disdained then of rings prince, that he that wide-flyer with troop should seek, with great army; not he for himself the fight dreaded, nor for himself of the serpent's prowess as anything regarded, strength and courage, because he before many straits braving of assaults survived, of battle-clashes, after he Hrothgar's, victory-blessed man, hall cleansed, and in battle destroyed Grendel's kinsmen of hateful race.
