# sent_id = beowulf.00669
# text = Dyde him of healse hring gyldenne þīoden þrīsthȳdig, þegne gesealde, geongum gārwigan, goldfāhne helm, bēah ond byrnan, hēt hyne brūcan well--: 'þū eart endelāf ūsses cynnes, Wǣgmundinga; ealle wyrd forswēop mīne māgas tō metodsceafte, eorlas on elne; ic him æfter sceal.'
# ref = 2809-2816
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = The stout-hearted lord took from his neck the golden ring and gave it to his retainer, the young spear-warrior: the gold-adorned helmet, the necklace and the mail-coat. He bade him use them well: 'You are the last remnant of our race, the Wægmundings; fate has swept away all my kinsmen to their destined end, warriors in their courage; I must follow after them.'
# literal_translation = Did he from neck ring golden, lord stout-hearted, to retainer gave, to young spear-fighter, gold-adorned helmet, necklace and mail-coat; ordered him to enjoy well: 'You are last-remnant of our race, of Wægmundings; all fate swept away my kinsmen to destiny, warriors in courage; I after them must.'
