# sent_id = beowulf.00681
# text = Wīglāf maðelode, Wēohstānes sunu, sec[g] sārigferð -- seah on unlēofe--: 'þæt, lā, mæg secgan sē ðe wyle sōð specan, þæt se mondryhten, sē ēow þā māðmas geaf, ēoredgeatwe, þē gē þǣr on standað, -- þonne hē on ealubence oft gesealde healsittendum helm ond byrnan, þēoden his þegnum, swylce hē þrȳðlīcost ōwer feor oððe nēah findan meahte--, þæt hē gēnunga gūðgewǣdu wrāðe forwurpe, ðā hyne wīg beget.
# ref = 2862-2872
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Wiglaf spoke, Weohstan's son, a man sad at heart — he looked at the unloved one —: 'Indeed, that one can say, who wishes to speak the truth, that the lord who gave you those treasures, the war-gear in which you stand there — when he often gave on the ale-bench to hall-sitters helmet and mail-coat, a prince to his retainers, such as he, most gloriously, could find anywhere far or near — that he completely threw away the war-garments in senselessness, when war befell him.'
# literal_translation = Wiglaf spoke, Weohstan's son, man sad-at-heart — looked on the unloved —: 'that, lo, may say he who will truth speak, that the lord, who you those treasures gave, war-gear, which you there on stand — when he on ale-bench often gave hall-sitters helmet and mail-coat, prince his retainers, such as he most-gloriously anywhere far or near find could —, that he completely war-garments senselessly threw-away, when him war befell.'
