# sent_id = beowulf.00710
# text = Ne scel ānes hwæt meltan mid þām mōdigan, ac þǣr is māðma hord, gold unrīme grimme gecēa(po)d, ond nū æt sīðestan sylfes fēore bēagas (geboh)te; þā sceall brond fretan, ǣled þeccean,-- nalles eorl wegan māððum tō gemyndum, nē mægð scȳne habban on healse hringweorðunge, ac sceal geōmormōd, golde berēafod oft nalles ǣne elland tredan, nū se herewīsa hleahtor ālegde, gamen ond glēodrēam.
# ref = 3010-3021
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# base_analysis = Giuseppe Brunetti
# dependency_model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Not only a part shall melt away with the brave one, but there is a hoard of treasures, countless gold, grimly buried, and now at the last he bought rings with his own life; then fire shall devour, flame enfold — not at all shall a warrior wear treasure in remembrance, nor may a beautiful woman have a ring-adornment around her neck, but, sad of mind, despoiled of gold, she must often, not once only, tread a foreign land, now that the army-leader has laid aside laughter, revelry and joyful mirth.
# literal_translation = Not shall one part melt with the courageous one, but there is of treasures a hoard, gold countless, grimly buried, and now at the last with (his) own life rings bought; then shall fire devour, flame enfold — not at all a warrior wear treasure in remembrance, nor may a woman beautiful have around neck a ring-adornment, but shall sad-of-mind, of gold despoiled, often not once a foreign land tread, now that the army-leader laughter laid aside, revelry and joyful mirth.
